WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.113 --> 00:00:12.961
Picture a scene from the Indiana Jones adventure movie trilogy: The hero, a modern adventurer and archaeologist named

2
00:00:13.101 --> 00:00:16.523
Indiana Jones, is in the tropical jungles of India.

3
00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:24.469
He fearlessly climbs a tall cliff, travels through the rain forest, dodges poison arrows, and crosses a rope

4
00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:32.137
bridge. Despite their extreme exhaustion and the drizzling rain, Jones and his team keep walking through the mountains.

5
00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:38.540
Their destination is a ridge, reported to be the location of the ancient ruins of the "Temple of

6
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.542
Doom." The men stop to rest at a small clearing.

7
00:00:43.482 --> 00:00:46.884
Indiana Jones notices the rocks are unusually flat.

8
00:00:47.844 --> 00:00:52.606
He pulls away a vine to reveal a stone wall carved with words and pictures.

9
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:59.665
All the men cheer, and Indiana cheers the loudest—at last they have found the Temple of Doom.

10
00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:04.086
Is this story of Indiana Jones fact or fiction?

11
00:01:04.106 --> 00:01:06.087
"Fiction, of course!"

12
00:01:06.307 --> 00:01:07.687
would be the correct answer.

13
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:16.349
But the inspiration for the daredevil explorer Indiana Jones was a real-life person, the American archaeologist and explorer

14
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.550
Hiram Bingham.

15
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:24.832
Bingham had many adventures as he explored the jungles of South America in the early twentieth century.

16
00:01:26.228 --> 00:01:33.892
However, Bingham's greatest achievement was in 1911, when he made one of the most important archaeological discoveries of

17
00:01:33.932 --> 00:01:36.113
the century, Machu Picchu.

18
00:01:37.473 --> 00:01:41.835
Machu Picchu is a city in the Andes Mountains of Peru, South America.

19
00:01:43.076 --> 00:01:47.878
It was built by the Inca people between 1440 and 1470.

20
00:01:49.298 --> 00:01:55.034
It is sometimes called the Lost City of the Incas because it remained hidden in the jungle for

21
00:01:55.154 --> 00:01:56.535
over four hundred years.

22
00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:04.882
Machu Picchu is extraordinary because it is so well preserved and because it shows the exceptional skills and

23
00:02:04.962 --> 00:02:06.163
knowledge of the Incas.

24
00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:12.529
The Incas were a tribe who lived near the city of Cuzco, Peru, in the twelfth century.

25
00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:20.576
From the twelfth century to the mid-sixteenth century, the Inca Empire expanded and encompassed most of South America.

26
00:02:21.716 --> 00:02:27.678
The Incas were able to rule this territory because their society was very regimented and productive.

27
00:02:28.658 --> 00:02:38.481
The Incas developed highly sophisticated agriculture, architecture, engineering, government administration, education, health care, and fabric-making technology.

28
00:02:39.541 --> 00:02:44.083
Incan buildings were stable and able to withstand the region's frequent earthquakes.

29
00:02:45.063 --> 00:02:51.590
Temples were constructed completely without mortar; instead the stones were cut to fit together exactly.

30
00:02:52.751 --> 00:02:58.134
Almost five hundred years later, Incan forts and temples stand in near-perfect condition.

31
00:02:59.314 --> 00:03:03.977
No other civilization in the world could cut and assemble stone blocks so perfectly.

32
00:03:05.498 --> 00:03:09.901
The biggest concern for the Incan government was feeding its massive empire.

33
00:03:11.281 --> 00:03:15.504
Growing crops was difficult in the high and rocky terrain of the Andes Mountains.

34
00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:24.603
However, the Incas designed farmland for corn, potatoes, and medicinal plants by shaping their hills into level strips

35
00:03:24.663 --> 00:03:25.624
of fertile ground.

36
00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:32.970
These terraces were so successful that in the fifteenth century, there was more land being cultivated in the

37
00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.391
area than there is today.

38
00:03:35.572 --> 00:03:42.378
Recently archaeologists set up sample crops using Incan farming methods and produced more food per acre than they

39
00:03:42.418 --> 00:03:43.800
could with modern methods.

40
00:03:45.212 --> 00:03:48.575
Incan government and education was also highly organized.

41
00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:54.799
All citizens in the Inca Empire aged 13 were required to take an intelligence test.

42
00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.584
If they failed the test, they were trained in a trade, and if they passed the test, they

43
00:04:00.624 --> 00:04:03.266
were sent to Cuzco and became public servants.

44
00:04:04.647 --> 00:04:09.630
It is believed that a few of these brilliant public servants were selected to work at Machu Picchu

45
00:04:10.031 --> 00:04:11.752
as assistants to the royal family.

46
00:04:12.936 --> 00:04:19.942
A position at Machu Picchu was special; it is estimated that a maximum of only 1,200 people resided

47
00:04:19.982 --> 00:04:21.303
there at any one time.

48
00:04:22.304 --> 00:04:29.210
The Incan royal family and their guests spent time there, but most residents were workers, including potters, stonemasons,

49
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.492
silversmiths, goldsmiths, and weavers.

50
00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:39.054
Some experts believe that Machu Picchu was a summer retreat for Incan nobility and royalty because the Incan

51
00:04:39.174 --> 00:04:43.955
emperor at the time, Pachacuti, enjoyed its incredible natural beauty.

52
00:04:45.136 --> 00:04:51.037
But Machu Picchu was an attractive place for other reasons too; the weather was cool, and it was

53
00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:52.358
quiet and isolated.

54
00:04:53.558 --> 00:05:00.760
These factors made it an ideal place for recreation, reflection, and conducting business during the hot Peruvian summers.

55
00:05:02.288 --> 00:05:06.650
Other people think that the site was chosen because of its high altitude and isolation.

56
00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:11.032
It provided the perfect location for a secure military base.

57
00:05:12.112 --> 00:05:18.695
The royal family could use Machu Picchu to escape from any armed conflicts occurring below in Cuzco and

58
00:05:18.755 --> 00:05:22.457
also observe the Peruvian landscape for any potential threats.

59
00:05:23.937 --> 00:05:29.580
Machu Picchu consists of over two hundred stone buildings built in typically precise Incan fashion.

60
00:05:30.548 --> 00:05:36.031
In fact, the stone blocks were cut so exactly that it is impossible to slide even a credit

61
00:05:36.072 --> 00:05:36.932
card between them.

62
00:05:38.093 --> 00:05:44.597
The site features three distinct areas: the religious area, the military area, and the residential area.

63
00:05:45.457 --> 00:05:47.879
They are separated by a majestic square.

64
00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:55.784
The religious area has temples and a sundial known as the Intihuatana, or "sun-catcher," shaped out of natural

65
00:05:55.844 --> 00:06:01.374
rock. It was also used as an altar to worship the Incan sun god, Inti.

66
00:06:02.474 --> 00:06:06.816
A priest or the Incan king would give speeches next to the Intihuatana.

67
00:06:07.836 --> 00:06:13.219
The acoustics in the main square below are perfect, comparable to those of modern auditoriums.

68
00:06:14.519 --> 00:06:21.082
Machu Picchu was inhabited for about one hundred years, but its location was so remote that few people

69
00:06:21.142 --> 00:06:22.622
were aware of its existence.

70
00:06:23.723 --> 00:06:28.830
The Spanish conquered Peru in 1532, but they never found Machu Picchu.

71
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:37.276
Over the next four hundred years, only a handful of farmers lived in this isolated and forgotten place.

72
00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:42.581
In 1911 Machu Picchu was reintroduced to the rest of the world by Hiram Bingham.

73
00:06:43.722 --> 00:06:49.466
Bingham and his team had set out from Lima, the capital of Peru, on an archaeological expedition.

74
00:06:50.587 --> 00:06:56.352
They were searching for Vilcabamba and Vitcos, two old Incan cities rumored to be hidden in the jungle.

75
00:06:58.024 --> 00:07:04.607
Bingham's expedition traveled on foot and by mule through the Urubamba Valley and into the Urubamba Gorge.

76
00:07:05.787 --> 00:07:09.489
There he met local people who had heard about some old ruins nearby.

77
00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:15.051
They led Hiram Bingham to the ruins of Machu Picchu in November of that year.

78
00:07:15.951 --> 00:07:21.694
Initially Bingham was struck by the beauty and majesty of the city: "In the variety of its charms

79
00:07:21.914 --> 00:07:26.276
and the power of its spell," he wrote, "I know of no place in the world which can

80
00:07:26.336 --> 00:07:27.176
compare with it."

81
00:07:28.176 --> 00:07:33.679
Bingham made several trips back to Machu Picchu and took over eleven thousand photos of the site.

82
00:07:34.859 --> 00:07:43.163
In 1913 the site received a significant amount of publicity after National Geographic magazine devoted its entire April

83
00:07:43.223 --> 00:07:44.884
1913 issue to it.

84
00:07:46.124 --> 00:07:52.347
Later Bingham wrote a number of books and articles about Machu Picchu; his book Lost City of the

85
00:07:52.447 --> 00:07:56.340
Incas became a best seller.

86
00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:01.424
Today Machu Picchu is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a popular tourist attraction.

87
00:08:02.545 --> 00:08:06.649
In 2003 four hundred thousand people visited Machu Picchu.

88
00:08:07.649 --> 00:08:11.492
Despite its remote location, it is easy for a tourist to visit.

89
00:08:12.353 --> 00:08:14.615
First, fly to Peru's capital, Lima.

90
00:08:15.476 --> 00:08:20.319
Then, from Lima, take another short flight to the city of Cuzco in the southern part of the

91
00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:27.126
country. Once in Cuzco, you can hire a guide and hike to Machu Picchu along the Incan Trail.

92
00:08:28.066 --> 00:08:30.046
This takes about four days on foot.

93
00:08:30.947 --> 00:08:37.548
Alternatively you can take a short train ride to Aguas Calientes, the nearest town to Machu Picchu, and

94
00:08:37.588 --> 00:08:40.409
then take a twenty-minute bus ride up to the ruins.

95
00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:47.371
Either way you will arrive at the breathtaking Machu Picchu, a sight like nothing else you will ever

96
00:08:47.471 --> 00:08:48.131
experience!
